//? Title: Look-Ahead, Coordinate Steps and Nonlinear Conjugate Gradient
//? Subtitle: Exact request points, rotated curvature and coupled coordinates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A smooth objective may be easy along one coordinate system and difficult along
another. Momentum, coordinate updates and conjugate directions exploit different
structure. We inspect exact early steps before comparing Nesterov look-ahead,
cyclic coordinate descent, randomized blocks and nonlinear Polak–Ribiere-plus CG
on diagonal, rotated, coupled and Rosenbrock objectives. Callback counts and final
gradient norms qualify every finite-budget comparison.
)
\toc

> The method

Classical momentum evaluates the gradient at x. This Nesterov implementation instead
uses a look-ahead point x+mu*v, then v_next=mu*v-eta*gradient(x+mu*v) and
x_next=x+v_next. A gradient-request curve can therefore show points that are not
returned iterates. With fixed eta/mu this is not every acceleration schedule for
strongly convex problems, and no universal rate is inferred from these runs.

CoordinateDescent visits each coordinate in order, requests a full gradient, and
backtracks a partial-gradient step until Armijo decrease holds. It does **not**
solve the coordinate's exact one-dimensional minimisation problem. An exhausted
search restores that coordinate. A full gradient at epoch end supplies its stop
test. RandomizedBlockCoordinateDescent reshuffles all coordinates once per epoch,
partitions them into blocks and performs simultaneous block steps; it samples
permutations, rather than independent coordinate indices with replacement.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_coordinate_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_coordinate_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_coordinate_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_coordinate_mechanism.svg build/opt_coordinate_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_coordinate_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_coordinate_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The methods differ in where they request derivatives, which directions they use and how they select steps.)
\alttext(A current or look-ahead point supplies derivatives. Axis, block or conjugate directions propose a step, which is updated directly or checked by a line search.)

For nonlinear conjugate gradient, start d_0=-g_0 and use the implemented PR-plus
coefficient, with negative values clipped and a descent fallback/restart:

$$
 \beta_t=\max\!\left(0,\frac{g_t^T(g_t-g_{t-1})}{g_{t-1}^Tg_{t-1}}\right),
 \qquad d_t=-g_t+\beta_t d_{t-1}.
$$
\alttext(Polak–Ribiere-plus divides the current gradient dotted with its change by the previous squared gradient norm, clips negative results to zero, and combines the negative gradient with the old direction.)

Armijo backtracking chooses a decreasing step. This is nonlinear CG, not linear
CG with exact line minimisation: it does not guarantee a p-step solution to a
p-dimensional quadratic. All four methods use O(p) direction/state storage beyond
the objective. Cyclic and block epochs can request several full gradients and
multiple trial values; equal epoch budgets are not equal work or elapsed time.

> Small worked examples

>> Look-ahead points and inexact coordinate minimisation

Take f=x0 squared+5*x1 squared, starting at (1,2). With eta=.05 and mu=.9,
Nesterov's first displacement is (-.1,-1), giving (.9,1). Its next gradient is
requested at (.81,.1), not at (.9,1), and the second returned point is (.729,.05).
The native trace prints both kinds of request.

Cyclic coordinate descent starts trial rates at one and halves them. It reaches
x0=zero using a .5 step, but accepts x1=-.5 at rate .125, rather than solving
that coordinate exactly at rate .1. The one-epoch result has loss 1.25. For CG,
the exact first line-minimising rate would be 404/4008, whereas the Armijo grid
accepts .125. The two-step result is checked numerically rather than assuming
finite-dimensional exact termination.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_coordinate_requests.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective                 f({2, 10}, {0, 0});
  o::NesterovAcceleratedGradientOptions n;
  n.step_size      = .05;
  n.max_iterations = 2;
  n.tolerance      = 0;
  const auto nag =
      o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::NesterovAcceleratedGradient(n), {1, 2});
  o::CoordinateDescentOptions c;
  c.max_iterations = 1;
  c.tolerance      = 0;
  const auto cd = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::CoordinateDescent(c), {1, 2});
  o::ConjugateGradientOptions g;
  g.max_iterations = 2;
  g.tolerance      = 0;
  const auto cg = o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::ConjugateGradient(g), {1, 2});
  const auto v = nag.trace.values(), look = nag.trace.sample(f, "gradient");
  auto       geometry =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(
              v.numeric_column("x0").values(),
              v.numeric_column("x1").values(),
              "value points",
              {40, 100, 190})
          .points(
              look.numeric_column("x0").values(),
              look.numeric_column("x1").values(),
              "gradient requests",
              {190, 100, 35},
              4)
          .title("Nesterov: value versus look-ahead")
          .x_label("x0")
          .y_label("x1")
          .x_limits(0, 1.2)
          .y_limits(0, 2.8)
          .legend({"value points", "gradient requests"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  auto trials = o::optimization_comparison(f, {cg}, {"CG trials"})
                    .title("CG value calls include rejected trials");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(geometry)
      .add(trials)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/opt_coordinate_requests.svg");
  std::print(
      "Nesterov\n\n{}\n\nGradient request points\n\n{}\n\nOne coordinate epoch\n\n{}\n\nTwo CG "
      "steps\n\n{}\n",
      nag.summary().to_md(7),
      look.to_md(7),
      cd.summary().to_md(7),
      cg.summary().to_md(7));
}
```
